questions.stem:
A study hall keeps its seating chart in a two-dimensional array and prints it:
int[][] seats = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}};
for (int r = 0; r < seats.length; r++)
    for (int c = 0; c < seats[r].length; c++)
        System.out.print(seats[r][c] + " ");
What exact sequence of digits reaches the screen?

A cipher table is read down its columns instead of across its rows:
int[][] g = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}};
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int c = 0; c < g[0].length; c++)
    for (int r = 0; r < g.length; r++)
        sb.append(g[r][c]);
System.out.println(sb);
What is printed?

A price board is dumped and then fails on a machine that only ever shows six values:
int[][] g = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}};
for (int r = 0; r < g.length; r++)
    for (int c = 0; c < g[0].length; c++)
        System.out.print(g[c][r] + " ");
What actually happens when this runs?

A runner reads the leading time from each lane of a results grid:
int[][] g = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}, {5, 6}};
int sum = 0;
for (int[] row : g) sum += row[0];
System.out.println(sum);
What is printed?

A developer wants to see a small grid in the console and writes:
import java.util.Arrays;
int[][] g = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};
System.out.println(Arrays.toString(g));
The program compiles and runs. What appears on the console?

A report dumps a jagged grid and then dies partway through the second row:
int[][] g = {{5, 6, 7}, {8, 9}};
for (int r = 0; r < g.length; r++)
    for (int c = 0; c < g[0].length; c++)
        System.out.print(g[r][c] + " ");
What does the run produce?

A timetable has one row that received no entries at all:
int[][] g = {{1, 2}, {}, {3}};
int total = 0;
for (int r = 0; r < g.length; r++)
    for (int c = 0; c < g[r].length; c++)
        total += g[r][c];
System.out.println(total);
What is printed?

A high-jump roster records three differently sized attempts lists and reports each day's best mark:
int[][] g = {{4, 9, 2}, {7}, {1, 8}};
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int[] row : g) {
    int max = row[0];
    for (int v : row) if (v > max) max = v;
    sb.append(max);
}
System.out.println(sb);
What is printed?

A two-slot checker is filled by hand, and both slots end up pointing at the same row:
int[][] a = new int[2][];
a[0] = new int[]{1, 2, 3};
a[1] = a[0];
a[1][2] = 99;
System.out.println(a[0][2]);
What is printed?

A guard method must reject a grid that contains a missing row, without throwing while doing so:
static boolean usable(int[][] data) {
    for (int r = 0; r < data.length; r++) {
        if (______________________) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
Which line fills the blank so the method reports a null row instead of crashing?

A parser reads rows of a declared width and wants the index of the first row that came in too narrow:
static int firstBadRow(int[][] data, int width) {
    for (int r = 0; r < data.length; r++) {
        if (______________________) {
            return r;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
Called with data = {{1,2,3},{4},{5,6,7}} and width 3, which blank makes the method return 1?

A builder must turn a list of per-row widths into a grid whose rows differ in size:
static int[][] build(int[] counts) {
    int[][] grid = new int[counts.length][];
    for (int r = 0; r < counts.length; r++) {
        ______________________;
    }
    return grid;
}
Which line allocates row r with its own requested width?

A checker compares every row against the first:
static boolean rectangular(int[][] data) {
    if (data == null || data.length == 0) return false;
    for (int r = 1; r < data.length; r++)
        if (data[r] == null || data[r].length != data[0].length) return false;
    return true;
}
System.out.println(rectangular(new int[][]{{1,2},{3,4},{5,6,7}}));
What does the program print?

A lights-out rule counts the four orthogonal neighbours of a cell, guarding each side:
int[][] grid = {{1,0,1},{0,0,0},{1,0,1}};
int r = 0, c = 1;
int live = 0;
if (r > 0 && grid[r-1][c] == 1) live++;
if (r < grid.length-1 && grid[r+1][c] == 1) live++;
if (c > 0 && grid[r][c-1] == 1) live++;
if (c < grid[0].length-1 && grid[r][c+1] == 1) live++;
System.out.println(live);
What is printed?

A vertical neighbour count is written without any bounds guards:
int[][] grid = {{1,1},{1,1}};
int r = 1, c = 0;
int live = 0;
if (grid[r-1][c] == 1) live++;
if (grid[r+1][c] == 1) live++;
System.out.println(live);
What happens?

A neighbour count starts at the top-left corner and omits the lower bound checks:
int[][] grid = {{1,1},{1,1}};
int r = 0, c = 0;
int live = 0;
if (grid[r-1][c] == 1) live++;
if (grid[r+1][c] == 1) live++;
System.out.println(live);
What happens?

A rule counts the eight surrounding cells of the middle cell of a 3-by-3 block:
int[][] grid = {{0,0,0},{0,1,0},{0,0,0}};
int live = 0;
for (int dr = -1; dr <= 1; dr++)
    for (int dc = -1; dc <= 1; dc++) {
        if (dr == 0 && dc == 0) continue;
        int r = 1 + dr, c = 1 + dc;
        if (r >= 0 && r < grid.length && c >= 0 && c < grid[0].length && grid[r][c] == 1) live++;
    }
System.out.println(live);
What is printed?

A horizontal neighbour count borrows its bounds from the first row:
int[][] grid = {{1,1,1},{1},{1,1}};
int r = 1, c = 2;
int live = 0;
if (c > 0 && grid[r][c-1] == 1) live++;
if (c < grid[0].length-1 && grid[r][c+1] == 1) live++;
System.out.println(live);
What happens?

A row is duplicated by assignment and then edited:
int[] row = {1, 2, 3};
int[] copy = row;
copy[0] = 99;
System.out.println(row[0]);
What is printed?

A grid is copied with clone and then edited through the copy:
int[][] a = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};
int[][] b = a.clone();
b[0][0] = 99;
System.out.println(a[0][0] + "," + b[0][0]);
What is printed?

A builder collects rows and then keeps editing the row it just added:
int[] row = {1, 2, 3};
java.util.ArrayList<int[]> rows = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
rows.add(row);
row[0] = 99;
System.out.println(rows.get(0)[0]);
What is printed?
